narrative:
## A Weather Conversion Toolkit

Converting between temperature scales is a classic programming exercise — and genuinely useful.

### Celsius to Fahrenheit

```xiom
fn c_to_f(celsius: Float64) -> Float64 {
  return celsius * 9.0 / 5.0 + 32.0;
}

fn f_to_c(fahrenheit: Float64) -> Float64 {
  return (fahrenheit - 32.0) * 5.0 / 9.0;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(c_to_f(0.0));    // 32.0 (freezing)
  io.println(c_to_f(100.0));  // 212.0 (boiling)
  io.println(f_to_c(98.6));   // 37.0 (body temp)
  return 0;
}
```

### State-Checking Functions

Combine your converters with boolean checks:

```xiom
fn is_freezing(celsius: Float64) -> Bool {
  return celsius <= 0.0;
}

fn is_boiling(celsius: Float64) -> Bool {
  return celsius >= 100.0;
}

fn is_room_temp(celsius: Float64) -> Bool {
  return celsius >= 18.0 && celsius <= 25.0;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(is_freezing(-5.0));    // true
  io.println(is_boiling(105.0));    // true
  io.println(is_room_temp(22.0));   // true
  return 0;
}
```

### A Complete Weather Report

```xiom
fn weather_report(celsius: Float64) {
  io.println("Celsius: ");
  io.println(celsius);
  io.println("Fahrenheit: ");
  io.println(c_to_f(celsius));
  if is_freezing(celsius) {
    io.println("Status: FREEZING!");
  } else if is_boiling(celsius) {
    io.println("Status: BOILING!");
  } else {
    io.println("Status: Normal");
  }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  weather_report(30.0);
  return 0;
}
```

One input, rich output — that's the power of composing small functions into a larger report.

